DOES THE RIDGE TRACK THE DWELL?
  dwell       1      2      3      4      5      6      8     10     13
  M=2      1.15   1.15   1.16  1.51*   0.92   1.06   1.11   1.12   1.11   peak at fast=4
  M=3      1.12   1.12   1.11   1.19  1.48*   0.93   1.04   1.25   1.09   peak at fast=5
  M=5      1.05   1.15   1.14   1.06   1.11  1.20*   0.94   1.08   1.12   peak at fast=6
  M=8      1.12   1.08   1.13   1.13   1.13   1.22   1.16  1.53*   1.03   peak at fast=10
  M=13     1.17   1.01   1.09   1.20   1.14   1.21   1.12   1.24  1.30*   peak at fast=13
  * = best lift for that dwell, maximised over all slow windows.

  The ridge MOVES WITH THE DWELL. That is the mechanism: an M-bar mean's slope
  turns over exactly the M bars the confirmation is counting, so the signal is
  reading the same window the dwell reads, not leading it. It cannot bridge a
  delay it is measuring from the inside -- which is why the lift is large and
  the surrogate reproduces nearly all of it.

